"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/iec-achieves-91-completion-rate-local-election-results","The Electoral Commission (IEC) has achieved a 91% national result completion rate for the 2026 Local Government Elections as at 5 pm on Friday. “The Electoral Commission reports that it continues to make significant progress in finalising the results for the 2026 Local Government Elections,” the Commission said. The Commission expects to receive a total of 66 073 result slips from 23 696 voting stations. Results have been finalised in 150 of the 213 municipalities, excluding the 44 District Councils. All municipalities in the Northern Cape have complete results. Completion rates by province are as follows: The Commission said the remaining results are mostly in metropolitan municipal areas, where population density means there are more voting districts. It also noted that participation by contestants has resulted in longer ballots and result slips. “The Electoral Commission reminds all stakeholders that results remain provisional until all objections and verification processes have been concluded,” the Commission said. It urged political parties, independent candidates, the media and the public to rely only on officially verified results published by the Commission. The Commission also dismissed social media claims that results for Wards 4 and 23 in Emalahleni Local Municipality, Mpumalanga, were being withheld because ballots cast exceeded the number of registered voters. “This claim is false because the official voter turnout in the cited wards is just over 50%, well below the registered voter population,” the Commission said. It also said another claim from the same source, that a municipal official voted four times, was misleading. According to the Commission, the voter went to the wrong voting district in her ward, was scanned there, and was then directed to her correct voting district. “Voting more than once is not possible in the 2026 Local Government Elections because a voter may only cast a ballot in the voting district in which they are registered,” it said. The Commission has also called on citizens to verify social media claims before sharing them, warning that spreading false information can breach the Electoral Code of Conduct and the associated Disinformation Code. It reiterated its committed to finalising results as quickly as possible while safeguarding the accuracy, transparency and integrity of the electoral process. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2541,"91%, 50%",2
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/gcis-dg-visits-roc-observe-election-results-process","Government Communication and Information System (GCIS) Director-General Lloyd Nedohe has visited the National Results Operations Centre (ROC) in Midrand to observe the processing and counting of votes following the 2026 Local Government Elections. During his visit, Nedohe met with GCIS officials deployed at the centre, who have been supporting government communication efforts during the electoral process. He expressed his appreciation for the work being carried out at the ROC and commended the pace at which the vote-counting process was progressing. The visit also provided an opportunity to engage with GCIS staff on the ground and acknowledge their contribution to ensuring that citizens remain informed about the electoral process. Reflecting on the elections, Nedohe described voter participation as encouraging, particularly the involvement of young people who came out to exercise their democratic rights. He commended the millions of people who turned out to vote, noting that the participation of young people, including first-time voters, was particularly encouraging. He observed that the previous elections had taken place under the difficult circumstances of the COVID-19 pandemic, making the current electoral experience different. Nedohe also highlighted the excitement expressed by young people who were voting for the first time, saying GCIS had engaged some of them through vox pops during the election period. He said many had shared positive experiences about participating in the democratic process for the first time. The elections saw the involvement of various stakeholders, including the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC), government departments, and law enforcement agencies, working together to support the electoral process. -SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",1929,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/guidelines-strengthen-coalition-governance","Government is preparing guidelines for coalition negotiations and arrangements following the 2026 Local Government Elections. The guidelines are intended to promote stability and ensure that political disagreements do not undermine service delivery. This is according to Government Deputy Spokesperson William Baloyi, who spoke to SAnews at the Electoral Commission of South Africa's (IEC) National Results Operations Centre (ROC) in Midrand on Friday. Baloyi said the guidelines, which will be released through the Department of Cooperative Governance and Traditional Affairs (CoGTA), will provide a framework for coalition negotiations and help prevent the instability and friction that have disrupted service delivery in some municipalities. “As government, there will not be issues of coalitions being characterised in some cities by friction that affects service delivery. Through CoGTA, we will be releasing guidelines on coalitions so that when coalition negotiations are going on, there are guidelines that guide them,” Baloyi said. He emphasised that political parties entering coalition arrangements must remember that their primary responsibility is to the communities that entrusted them with their votes. “The most important part is service delivery to the people, and all parties must remember that their contract with the voters is to improve their services,” he said. Baloyi said stable coalition arrangements are essential to keeping municipalities focused on their constitutional responsibilities and responsive to the needs of communities. He also called on residents to remain actively involved in local government beyond election day, noting that democratic participation includes holding elected representatives accountable for their campaign promises and service delivery commitments. Baloyi urged communities to attend public meetings convened by their elected representatives, raise concerns and receive feedback on issues affecting them. “When they call meetings, go to those meetings and hear how far the issues that were put there have progressed.” With voting concluded and citizens awaiting the results, Baloyi said the focus should now shift to translating the electoral mandate into improved services and greater accountability to communities. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2439,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/government-reiterates-warning-against-election-misinformation","Government has reiterated its call for South Africans and political parties to refrain from spreading misinformation and disinformation about the 2026 Local Government Elections. Speaking to SAnews at the Electoral Commission of South Africa's (IEC) Results Operations Centre (ROC) in Midrand on Friday, Government Deputy Spokesperson William Baloyi warned that the circulation of unverified results and unauthorised photographs of marked ballot papers undermines the integrity of the electoral process. Baloyi said the IEC remained the only legitimate authority mandated to announce election results. He urged the public to allow the results-capturing and verification processes to run their course. “We want to appeal that misinformation and disinformation are criminal acts. The South African Police Service has already indicated the number of people who have been arrested for misinformation and disinformation,” Baloyi told SAnews. He referred to the taking and sharing of photographs of oneself with a marked ballot paper as an example of conduct that contravenes electoral rules. Baloyi also expressed concern about people circulating speculative election results before being officially announced by the IEC, warning this can mislead the public. “The IEC is the legitimate structure that can pronounce all the results, and we want people to respect that,” he said. Baloyi also called on political parties and independent candidates to use the appropriate IEC mechanisms to raise complaints or objections, as opposed to issuing statements that might create unnecessary tension. He further urged all stakeholders to respect the outcome of the elections once the results have been finalised, saying these should be accepted as a reflection of the will of voters. Young people commended for participating in elections Meanwhile, Baloyi commended South Africans, particularly young people, for participating in the elections. Baloyi described the turnout as an important milestone in the country's democratic development. He highlighted the importance of local government elections, noting that municipalities are the sphere of government closest to communities and directly responsible for services that affect people's daily lives. While the IEC will provide the official voter turnout figures, Baloyi welcomed reports of increased youth participation, adding that more young people must be encouraged to take part in democratic processes. He also commended the security services and other stakeholders for their role in ensuring that the elections were generally incident-free, despite isolated challenges. Baloyi emphasised that voting is only one part of the democratic process, and citizens should continue to hold elected representatives accountable by attending public meetings and following up on service delivery commitments. “South Africa has once again demonstrated that it was a living democracy,” Baloyi said. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3090,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/shorter-queues-positive-voter-experiences-mark-2026-elections-hsrc","The 2026 Local Government Elections were characterised by shorter waiting times at voting stations and high levels of satisfaction with electoral officials, with 95% of voters expressing satisfaction with the quality of service provided by the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC). The findings are contained in the Human Sciences Research Council’s (HSRC) 2026 Election Satisfaction Survey (ESS), which assessed voters’ experiences of the electoral process on Election Day. Briefing the media on the findings, HSRC researcher, Dr Ben Roberts, said 91% of voters spent no more than 30 minutes queuing before casting their ballots, a marked improvement on the 2024 National and Provincial Elections. The HSRC noted that the improvement in shorter queuing times had reversed the increase experienced in 2024, returning to levels recorded during the 2021 Local Government Elections. The survey further found that 90% of voters travelled for no longer than 30 minutes to reach their voting stations, pointing to generally convenient access to voting facilities. “Voting was generally convenient; 90% travelled no more than 30 minutes (not shown), and 91% queued no longer than 30 minutes,” Dr Roberts said at the National Results Operations Centre (ROC) in Midrand. Satisfaction with the service provided by IEC officials remained high at 95%, broadly consistent with previous elections. In assessing the performance and conduct of electoral officials, voters gave positive ratings across six indicators, including helpfulness, patience, professionalism, friendliness, trustworthiness and impartiality. Helpfulness received the highest rating, with 79% of voters indicating that IEC officials demonstrated this quality to a great extent. Patience and professionalism each received 78%, while 77% rated officials as very friendly. Trustworthiness received a rating of 74%, while impartiality recorded the lowest rating, with 65% of voters indicating that officials were impartial to a great extent. The survey also found that 86% of voters, both those with disabilities and those without, considered voting stations accessible to elderly people and persons with disabilities. This reflected broadly comparable and overwhelmingly positive experiences across the two groups. However, the findings highlighted areas requiring further attention, particularly arrangements for special groups. “Most voters viewed arrangements for vulnerable groups positively, although assessments were less favourable for visually impaired voters, pregnant women and voters with babies (due mainly to uncertainty),” the Survey found. Regarding the voting procedures, 58% of voters said the procedures inside their voting stations were very easy to understand, while 38% described them as easy. Despite this positive assessment, information gaps remained, with 13% of voters indicating that they had far too little information about voting procedures and a further 13% saying they had too little information. Eastern Cape voters were the most likely to report having insufficient information, while Northern Cape voters were the least likely. The survey also found that most voters had little difficulty navigating the ballot papers. Some 93% of voters said it was easy to identify their preferred political party or candidate on the ballot papers, while 94% expressed satisfaction with the ballot papers. Voter education also featured in the assessment, with 60% of respondents describing the IEC's voter education campaign as very effective and 28% considering it somewhat effective. Radio and television emerged as the most useful sources of information and voter education, with 61% of voters rating each as very useful. Social media followed at 59%. The findings point to generally positive experiences of election administration, particularly in terms of the time taken to vote, the conduct of electoral officials and access to voting stations. However, the HSRC identified intimidation, impartiality, inclusive voting and information gaps as areas requiring targeted attention to further strengthen the electoral process. The 2026 ESS was conducted at 300 voting stations nationally, with fieldworkers interviewing randomly selected voters throughout Election Day. A total of 13 198 interviews were completed across all nine provinces, representing an 88% realisation rate, with a margin of error of less than 1%. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",4563,"95%, 91%, 90%, 90%, 91%, 95%, 79%, 78%, 77%, 74%, 65%, 86%, 58%, 38%, 13%, 13%, 93%, 94%, 60%, 28%, 61%, 59%, 88%, 1%",24
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/hsrc-survey-finds-voters-viewed-elections-free-and-fair","The overwhelming majority of voters regarded South Africa’s 2026 Local Government Elections as free and fair, with 90% expressing confidence that the electoral procedures were free and 91% satisfied that they were fair. This is according to the Human Sciences Research Council’s (HSRC) Election Satisfaction Survey (ESS) 2026, which assessed voters’ experiences and perceptions of the electoral process during the local government elections held on 4 November. Briefing the media on Friday at the National Results Operations Centre (ROC) in Midrand, HSRC researcher, Dr Ben Roberts, said the survey, conducted on behalf of the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC), found that perceptions of electoral integrity remained strong. The findings reflected continued public confidence in the country’s electoral processes. The results showed that 95% of voters were satisfied that their votes remained secret, consistent with the high levels of confidence in ballot secrecy recorded in previous elections. “Voter confidence in ballot secrecy remains exceptionally high and remarkably consistent over time. In 2026, 95% of voters were satisfied that their vote remained secret,” said Dr Roberts. Furthermore, 83% of voters indicated that they were either completely or very confident that their votes would be accurately counted. Despite these largely positive assessments of the electoral process, the survey highlighted a gradual decline in trust in the IEC. Trust in the Commission stood at 81% in 2026, down from 85% in 2024, 89% in 2021 and 93% in 2019. In 2016, it stood at 91%. Meanwhile, 5% of voters said they distrusted or strongly distrusted the Commission, while 14% were neutral, uncertain or declined to respond. The findings also revealed provincial differences in trust in the IEC. The Eastern Cape recorded the highest level of trust in the IEC at 87%, followed by the Northern Cape at 85%, the Free State at 84% and the Western Cape at 83%. Trust stood at 81% in Mpumalanga and 80% in Limpopo, while North West and Gauteng each recorded 79%. KwaZulu-Natal recorded the lowest level of trust at 72%. Voters who expressed confidence in the IEC cited its perceived professionalism, competence, fairness and impartiality, as well as accurate vote counting and the Commission’s record of administering elections. Positive personal experiences with electoral officials and confidence in ballot secrecy also featured prominently. One respondent expressed confidence in the Commission, saying: “I think the IEC has one of the most difficult jobs to do in this country and they do it well.” Other respondents cited the Commission’s perceived political neutrality and the protection of the vote as reasons for their trust. The 5% of voters who expressed distrust cited perceived corruption and dishonesty, doubts about the accuracy of vote counting, political bias and past controversies. “On perceived corruption and dishonesty, allegations of corruption, bribery and fraudulent practices are prominent reasons for distrust. Concerns about vote counting and electoral results: respondents express doubts about whether votes are accurately counted, properly accounted for and reflected in the final results,” said Dr Roberts. One respondent stated: “There is always corruption.” The survey also highlighted concerns about information circulating in the news and on social media. Some respondents said rumours and reports about previous electoral disputes had contributed to their mistrust. The findings suggest that, while voters retain considerable confidence in the electoral process, maintaining public trust remains important. The HSRC noted that positive experiences of the electoral process coexist with broader dissatisfaction with democracy and political institutions. The ESS is a representative survey of voters’ views and experiences on Election Day. The HSRC has conducted it since 1999. Its findings are used as one of the measures to determine whether elections in South Africa are free and fair. The results are also read out as part of the official election declaration. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",4257,"90%, 91%, 95%, 95%, 83%, 81%, 85%, 89%, 93%, 91%, 5%, 14%, 87%, 85%, 84%, 83%, 81%, 80%, 79%, 72%, 5%",21
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/kzn-iec-captures-88-election-results-slips","The Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) in KwaZulu-Natal has captured 88% of the expected election results slips, with 82% independently audited, as counting continues across the province. In an update on Friday, Provincial Electoral Officer Ntombifuthi Masinga said that of the 14 245 results slips expected from 5 028 voting stations across the province, 12 601 have been captured and 11 694 independently audited by 12pm. Masinga said the Commission continues to work through challenges affecting the results capturing process in several municipalities, including power failures, slow connectivity, staff fatigue and recounts. In eThekwini, high volumes and recounts have affected progress, although capturing has been ongoing since this morning, with 64% of results captured. In Msunduzi, the process has been delayed by morning rolling-back results slips recounts, with 40% of results captured. Capturing has also continued in Msinga, where storm-related power failures were reported, with progress at 70%. Abaqulusi and Ulundi reported staff fatigue due to slow connectivity, with 63% and 64% of results captured, respectively. In Ndwedwe, a storm-related power failure on Thursday, between 15:30 and 16:30, disrupted operations, prompting the relocation of results capturing to the KwaDukuza Library, which is being used as the KwaDukuza capturing site. The municipality has recorded 59% progress. Staff fatigue was also reported in Alfred Duma due to slow connectivity, with 49% of results captured, while Inkosi Mtubatuba has reached 67%, with power failures affecting connectivity. The Commission has reminded political parties, independent candidates, and members of the public that the deadline for lodging objections under Section 65 (S65) of the Electoral Act is 17:00 today. “This relates to instances where the objector believes that irregularities, which may have occurred at a voting station during voting or counting, may have compromised the credibility or integrity of the election,” Masinga said. The Commission will consider the objections lodged and issue determinations accordingly. Objections must be submitted directly to the Commission via email at S65objections@elections.org.za before the deadline. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2399,"88%, 82%, 64%, 40%, 70%, 63%, 64%, 59%, 49%, 67%",10
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/gauteng-disaster-teams-standby-severe-thunderstorms-loom","The Gauteng Department of Cooperative Governance and Traditional Affairs (CoGTA) has activated disaster management measures as severe thunderstorms are expected across most of the province on Friday afternoon. This follows a warning by the National Forecast Centre, also covering the south-western parts of the Mpumalanga Highveld. Affected districts, local and metropolitan municipalities include the City of Johannesburg, City of Tshwane, Dipaleseng, Ekurhuleni, Emalahleni, Govan Mbeki, Lekwa, Lesedi, Midvaal, Pixley Ka Seme and Victor Khanye. CoGTA said it had alerted provincial and municipal disaster management structures in the affected areas and called on municipalities to activate contingency plans and coordinate preparedness and response measures. “Municipalities are urged to ensure that disaster management teams, emergency services and relevant role-players are on standby, and to communicate timely warnings to communities. “The department further calls on municipalities to monitor low-lying areas, informal settlements and flood-prone roads, and to coordinate with provincial disaster management structures should conditions deteriorate. “CoGTA, through the National Disaster Management Centre, will continue to monitor the situation and support coordinated response efforts where required.” The storms are expected to bring strong winds, hail and heavy downpours. These conditions may cause flooding, damage to settlements and property, and localised disruptions to power supply and communications. Potential impacts include: Members of the public in the affected areas are urged to take precautions and follow safety advice. “If possible, stay indoors and away from metal objects. Do not seek shelter under trees or tall objects. Do not go fishing or play golf, as golf clubs and fishing rods are good conductors of electricity. Be aware that any combination of hail, strong winds and/or heavy downpours can be dangerous. Avoid crossing flooded roads, bridges and low-lying areas,” the department said. -SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2187,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/chikunga-pushes-accelerate-disability-employment","Government is stepping up efforts to expand employment opportunities for persons with disabilities, with public institutions and the private sector urged to accelerate employment equity targets and create more inclusive workplaces. Minister in the Presidency for Women, Youth and Persons with Disabilities, Sindisiwe Chikunga, made the call during the official launch of the South African Revenue Service (SARS) Deaf Accessibility and Cohort Programme in Pretoria on Friday. Chikunga commended SARS for introducing practical measures to improve communication access, skills development, and service delivery for deaf people. She said the initiative demonstrates how inclusion can be translated from principle into practice through employment, skills development, accessible communication, and institutional capability. The Minister noted that disability inclusion has been, and continues to be a constitutional imperative, supported by South Africa's legislative and international commitments, including the Constitution, the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities and the African Disability Protocol. She commended SARS for choosing to launch the initiative during Disability Rights Awareness Month (DRAM), observed from 3 November to 3 December. The observance will culminate on 3 December, when South Africa joins the international community in commemorating the International Day of Persons with Disabilities. READ | Chikunga calls for stronger partnerships to advance disability rights This year’s DRAM is taking place under the theme: “Creating Strategic Multi-Sectoral Partnerships for a Disability-Inclusive Society.” Chikunga said partnerships between government, the private sector and civil society are essential to removing barriers that prevent persons with disabilities from participating fully in social and economic life. She emphasised that inclusive workplaces must be supported by an inclusive education system, warning that barriers in schools often carry through into the workplace. These include inaccessible school infrastructure, inadequate assistive devices, a shortage of qualified South African Sign Language teachers and interpreters, as well as insufficient teacher training to support learners with disabilities. The Minister also highlighted the potential of technology, including artificial intelligence, automated captioning, sign language recognition, and speech-to-text tools, to improve access to education and employment. However, she warned that deliberate investment in devices, connectivity and training is necessary to ensure that these technologies benefit learners across all communities, rather than only those with access to resources. Collaboration and partnerships Chikunga proposed several areas of collaboration between her department and SARS, including developing a dedicated employment stream for persons with disabilities that can build on the experience of the Deaf Accessibility and Cohort Programme. She also called for partnerships between special schools and State-Owned Enterprises (SOEs), through which companies can use their Corporate Social Investment (CSI) budgets to address infrastructure and educational needs at special schools. “As part of this effort, we hope to bring together the CEOs of State-Owned Enterprises (SOEs) , Sector Education and Training Authorities (SETAs), and industry associations to commit a percentage of their Corporate Social Investment budgets towards turning around the state of special schools and improving the prospects of youth with disabilities. “SARS, while not strictly an SOE in the conventional sense, carries significant convening power across the public finance ecosystem. Let us also work together to make this project a reality,” the Minister said. Employment equity On employment equity, Chikunga said the National Development Plan had set a target of 7% representation of persons with disabilities by 2030. Cabinet has approved an annual increase of one percentage point in employment equity representation, with a target of 5% for the 2026/27 financial year. She said the department is leading by example, having achieved 5.56% representation. Chikunga warned that government departments and private sector organisations can no longer afford to treat employment equity as a compliance exercise. “Those who continue to pay lip service to employment equity will be named, and they will be shamed,” she said. She added that her department is working with organisations representing persons with disabilities to develop a credible database to help government departments and public entities identify and recruit suitably qualified candidates. The Minister further encouraged government departments, the private sector and society to procure goods and services from Supported Employment Enterprises, which are state-owned companies that provide employment opportunities for persons with disabilities. She spoke under the theme: “Every Hand has a Voice – Shaping an Inclusive Future.”– SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",5182,"7%, 5%, 56%",3
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/mpumalanga-powering-south-africas-future-through-skills","By Moahlodi Maphori The Mpumalanga province has powered South Africa for generations. Today, the province stands at the centre of the country’s Just Energy Transition, well placed to lead the way in building the skills and industries of a modern, diversified economy. This transition is opening a new chapter in which cleaner energy goes hand in hand with new skills, new industries and new opportunities for our people. Government is determined that these benefits reach every community. The province brings real strengths to this moment, because the experience built in its mines, power stations and factories has produced a workforce with deep technical knowledge and a proud industrial tradition. Those capabilities are valuable in the emerging economy of renewable energy, energy storage, advanced manufacturing, electric vehicles and green hydrogen, where artisans, technicians and engineers will be in demand, and the government is investing in preparing workers and young people to take up these opportunities. Government’s approach is guided by the principle that no one should be left behind, which means training is being designed around the needs of industry and in close partnership with business, labour, communities and training institutions. By aligning skills programmes with real economic opportunities and giving young people and existing workers clear pathways into apprenticeships, learnerships and employment, the government is ensuring that learning leads to work and that the transition delivers shared prosperity. This commitment took a significant step forward with the launch of the Mpumalanga Skills Development Zone on 29 October 2026, led by the Just Energy Transition (JET) Skills Desk and launched by the Minister of Higher Education and Training, Buti Manamela, together with the Mpumalanga Provincial Government. As the first of three zones under the national JET Skills Portfolio, it is anchored in Nkangala, with Emalahleni and Steve Tshwete taking part, and Nkangala TVET (Technical and Vocational Education and Training) College as the lead institution; it brings colleges, employers, government and communities together to plan skills around local needs, expand apprenticeships, learnerships and internships, and strengthen training facilities. The strength of this model lies in its link to the Special Economic Zones, which attract and concentrate industrial investment while the Skills Development Zones prepare local people to fill the jobs that investment creates. Working together, they allow skills planning to inform investment decisions and investment pipelines to guide what colleges teach, so that businesses can find the talent they need and residents can access the opportunities being created on their doorstep. Mpumalanga is therefore not simply a province adjusting to change but the place where South Africa’s next industrial chapter is being shaped, with government, business, labour and communities working side by side to turn the promise of the transition into lasting jobs, thriving local enterprises and a skilled workforce. By investing in people today, we are building an economy in which Mpumalanga continues to power the nation, and its people share fully in the growth that follows. * Maphori is the Economic Cluster Coordinator at the Government Communication and Information System (GCIS). General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3506,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/trial-seven-accused-rapper-akas-murders-adjourned-april","The trial of the seven men charged with the murders of popular musician Kiernan ‘AKA’ Forbes and Tebello ‘Tibz’ Motsoane has been adjourned by the Durban High Court to April 2027. The two were killed in a shooting outside a Durban restaurant in 2023. “The trial, which commenced in early October 2026, has seen the State lead evidence from more than 20 witnesses. Evidence presented thus far includes video footage, vehicle-tracking data, ballistic evidence and financial evidence. “The seven face several charges, including conspiracy to commit murder, two counts of murder, multiple counts of attempted murder, as well as firearm- and ammunition-related offences,” the National Prosecuting Authority said in a statement. The trial continues from 12 April to 14 May 2027, when the state will present further evidence. The seven accused remain in custody. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",1019,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/western-cape-reaffirms-commitment-agricultural-growth","The Western Cape government has reaffirmed its commitment to growing the agricultural sector through investment, innovation, skills development, export promotion and climate resilience. Western Cape Agriculture, Economic Development and Tourism MEC, Ivan Meyer, reiterated the commitment at the 31st Conference of the Royal Agricultural Society of the Commonwealth (RASC) held in Cape Town from 1 - 5 November 2026. Meyer said the conference provided an important platform to strengthen international agricultural partnerships and advance innovation, sustainability and food security. He welcomed delegates from across the Commonwealth, including RASC chairperson Sir Nicholas Bacon. In his address, Meyer highlighted agriculture's role in connecting people, economies and nations, while driving economic growth, employment, exports and rural development. “Agriculture has always been one of humanity's greatest connectors. It transcends borders, cultures and generations. It links producers to consumers, rural communities to global markets and innovation to opportunity,” Meyer said. He noted that Western Cape producers continue to compete successfully in some of the world's most demanding markets through innovation, sustainability and excellence. The MEC also acknowledged the challenges facing the global agricultural sector, including climate change, water scarcity, rising input costs and supply chain disruptions. However, he said technological advances, precision agriculture and climate-smart farming offer significant opportunities to strengthen the sector's resilience and competitiveness. “The question before us is not whether agriculture will change. The question is whether we will lead that change,” he said. Meyer emphasised the importance of collaboration across borders and sectors, noting that the Commonwealth's diverse agricultural community shares the common goals of improving livelihoods, protecting natural resources and building a sustainable future for generations to come. Against the backdrop of growing global divisions, he urged delegates to use agriculture as a catalyst for unity and cooperation. “In a world searching for common ground, perhaps our greatest task is not simply to make our economies stronger or our industries more competitive, but to make humanity great again. We do so when we choose cooperation over division, partnership over isolation and shared progress over narrow self-interest,” the MEC said. Meyer also commended Agri-Expo President Chris Fourie and the organisation for their role in the conference's organising committee and their longstanding contribution to agricultural development, innovation and market access in the Western Cape. He said Agri-Expo has, for nearly two centuries, played an important role in strengthening the competitiveness and sustainability of the province's agricultural sector. Reaffirming the province's commitment to creating an enabling environment for agricultural growth, Meyer said the Western Cape Government will continue to prioritise export support, skills development, climate resilience, and investment in innovation. “The Western Cape is open for business. We welcome investment. We welcome partnerships. We welcome innovation, and we welcome every opportunity to deepen our relationships with partners across the Commonwealth,” Meyer said. The conference brought together agricultural leaders, researchers, innovators and policymakers from across the Commonwealth to explore practical solutions to strengthen food systems, improve rural economies and advance sustainable agricultural development. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3767,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/hot-and-humid-weather-expected-kzn","The South African Weather Service (SAWS) has warned that hot, humid weather will create extremely uncomfortable conditions in north-eastern KwaZulu-Natal on Friday. The weather service said the province will experience partly cloudy and warm to hot conditions, with scattered showers and thundershowers. “It will become cloudy along the coast and adjacent interior in the afternoon. The wind along the coast will be moderate to fresh northerly but southerly to southwesterly in the south, becoming light to moderate south-easterly in the afternoon and spreading to Kosi Bay by the evening,” SAWS said. On Saturday and Sunday, the eastern parts of the country are expected to be partly cloudy and cool, with isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms. Widespread showers and thunderstorms are expected in KwaZulu-Natal. Elsewhere, conditions will be fine and warm to hot. The weather service has issued the following warnings for Friday: SAWS will continue to monitor weather conditions throughout the voting period and issue severe weather warnings and advisories as necessary. Members of the public are encouraged to follow official SAWS communication platforms for the latest weather updates. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",1363,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/20-nabbed-limpopo-election-related-offences","The South African Police Service (SAPS) in Limpopo has arrested 20 suspects for various election-related offences since the 2026 Local Government Elections began on Monday. Law enforcement agencies continue to enforce electoral laws and maintain law and order across the province to ensure a smooth election process. Approximately 33 cases linked to election-related incidents have been registered across Limpopo. These offences range from contravening the Electoral Act 73 of 1998, to fraud, common assault, malicious damage to property, and pointing a firearm. “Among the incidents reported were cases where individuals allegedly contravened the Electoral Act by taking photographs of marked ballot papers using their cellphones and subsequently circulating or publishing the images on various social media platforms. “Such conduct is prohibited in terms of electoral legislation and undermines the secrecy and integrity of the voting process,” the police said in a statement. Police also responded to other incidents involving alleged unlawful conduct during the election period, which resulted in the opening of criminal cases and the arrest of suspects. Lieutenant General Jan Scheepers, the Commissioner of Police in Limpopo, welcomed the arrests and commended SAPS members, other law enforcement agencies, and all stakeholders who worked tirelessly to ensure a safe and peaceful election period. He applauded the collective efforts, dedication, and cooperation shown by all role players, emphasising the importance of partnerships and coordinated strategies in safeguarding communities, voters and election officials. Scheepers expressed his appreciation for the members who remained committed to their duties throughout the election period and thanked stakeholders for their unwavering support, cooperation and prayers in ensuring the electoral process took place in a safe and secure environment. He reiterated that SAPS will not hesitate to take action against anyone who violates the law, regardless of their position or affiliation, and affirmed that all reported cases will be dealt with in accordance with the law. Some of the arrested suspects have already started appearing before their local magistrate's courts. - SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2393,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/lge2026-law-enforcement-alert-results-capturing-continues","South Africa’s law enforcement agencies remain on alert as the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) continues its work of counting, reconciling, verifying and capturing the results of the Local Government Elections held on Wednesday. This is according to the co-chairperson of the National Joint Operational and Intelligence Structure (NATJOINTS), Lieutenant General Tebello Mosikili, who briefed the media on Thursday evening. “Our operational responsibility … continues, with particular emphasis on protecting voting materials, securing results operations and safeguarding the integrity of the results management process. “NATJOINTS remains fully committed to sustaining its security posture until the 2026 Local Government Elections [period] is formally concluded. While we acknowledge the challenges and incidents encountered, the overall operational response demonstrates that the Security Cluster implemented its mandate with preparedness, discipline and determination,” Mosikili stated. READ | Police take action in Voting Day linked cases Reflecting on Voting Day itself, Mosikili said “coordinated deployment and decisive interventions” ensured a largely conducive environment, with minimal disruptions. Law enforcement adopted an intelligence-led and risk-informed approach to operations, with particular attention placed on several hotspots in KwaZulu-Natal, Western Cape, Mpumalanga and Gauteng. “Provincial Commissioners, national Directors-General and other senior officials provided strategic leadership and operational oversight to ensure that identified risks were effectively managed. “Eligible voters were able to exercise their democratic rights, while security forces maintained law and order and responded swiftly to reported incidents, and addressed emerging threats across all provinces. “This reflects the operational preparedness, institutional coordination and commitment of the Security Cluster to safeguarding the country’s democratic processes,” Mosikili said. Mosikili noted that planned marches and protests “did not materialise”. “There was no widespread disruption that compromised the electoral process. Where operational, logistical and service delivery-related challenges emerged at certain voting stations, law enforcement agencies intervened to stabilise the environment and facilitate the continuation of the voting process,” Mosikili stated. Results capturing and verification continue. READ | LGE2026: Results capturing making steady progress. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2655,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/n-west-legislature-urges-voters-collect-ids-voter-registration-weekend","The North West Provincial Legislature Deputy Speaker, Linah Miga, has encouraged voters across the province to collect their identity documents (IDs) at various offices of the Department of Home Affairs for voter registration weekend on 18 and 19 November 2023. This follows the establishment of the North West Provincial Home Affairs Stakeholder Forum, which serves as a mechanism for ensuring collaborations in addressing services related to the Department of Home Affairs. “Through engagements at this forum, it came to light that there are thousands of ID smart cards that have not been collected from the various district offices of the Department of Home Affairs,” said Miga. Citing the provincial report on uncollected IDs in the North West, Miga said there are 26 863 smart IDs in 15 district offices, as well as 684 green ID books that are still yet to be collected as of 6 November 2023. “Considering that a lower voter turnout was reported in the past elections, particularly in the North West, the legislature wishes to plead with all communities to visit the Home Affairs offices in their areas for collection of smart IDs. “The call to action is made urgent by the fact that voter registration for the 2024 general elections is scheduled for 18 and 19 November 2023 in the various stations across the country including the province. “It is, therefore, imperative for those who have not collected IDs to do so to embrace and honour their right to vote in the coming General Elections,” Miga said. The Provincial Home Affairs Stakeholders Forum encourages communities to visit their voting stations from 7:00 and 17:00 on Saturday and Sunday. “I further plead the citizens of the North West province to go in numbers and register to vote because it is your right.” – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",1940,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/g20-development-group-meetings-kick-under-sas-leadership","South Africa has reiterated its intention of using its G20 Presidency to foster global solidarity, equality and sustainable development. Minister in the Presidency for Monitoring, Planning and Evaluation, Maropene Ramokgopa, on Friday said her department has held its first meeting of the G20 Development Working Group. ""The Development Working Group recently held its first meeting and will continue in its meetings during the year to advance financing for development, social protection and global public goods. “South Africa is determined to utilise the Presidency of the G20 to contribute towards and foster global solidarity, equality and sustainable development,” Ramokgopa said. The Minister was addressing a briefing on progress made in the development of the Medium Term Development Plan (MTDP) 2024 – 2029, where she also highlighted work being done around the G20. South Africa's G20 Presidency runs from 1 December 2024 to 30 November 2025 -- only five years before the deadline of the United Nations (UN) 2030 Agenda. South Africa’s theme for this year’s G20 is: “Solidarity, Equality and Sustainable Development”. Ramokgopa said the theme aligns with South Africa’s development goals, and with global goals such as the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and the Pact of the Future, which was adopted at the 2024 Summit of the Future. The Minister issued a rallying call to all South Africans to support government’s efforts to implement the MTDP. The MTDP is a five-year strategic framework for development, inclusive growth and improved living conditions. It integrates the Government of National Unity’s Statement of Intent and the National Development Plan (NDP) into government’s priorities for the next five years. This plan is founded on credible evidence drawn from extensive research, and reflects a participatory process. Government intends to mobilise all stakeholders to collaborate and support the implementation of the MTDP 2024 – 2029 to ensure meaningful developmental progress for South Africa. “We call on all stakeholders in business, labour, civil society, academics, traditional leaders, and citizens, to work with government in advancing our goals of building a better world, and to ensure we continue to build our nation by implementing the MTDP to advance and sustain the development of our country,” Ramokgopa concluded. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2527,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/transnational-crimes-under-spotlight-regional-police-meeting","Law enforcement agencies within the Southern Africa region have committed to strengthening collaboration in the fight against cross-border and transnational organised crime. This is according to National Police Commissioner, General Fannie Masemola, who was speaking on Thursday at a two-day consultative meeting attended by heads of police in Botswana on transnational organised crime. This high-level meeting has brought together police chiefs and specialised detectives and intelligence operatives from Botswana, Namibia, Angola, Zambia, and Zimbabwe. Its aim was to ensure all law enforcement agencies within the region strengthen and heighten working relations through joint operations and investigations, explore new strategies and promote joint efforts to prevent and combat transnational organised crime. The meeting saw all six countries commit resources and capacity that will focus on dealing decisively with organised crime syndicates involved in stock theft, illegal mining, cybercrime, smuggling of stolen vehicles, the proliferation of firearms, drug trafficking as well as human trafficking and smuggling. “By coming together under one roof and sharing expertise, we are now better prepared, more capable, and one step ahead in confronting increasingly sophisticated criminal networks,” Masemola said. He told the delegates attending the meeting that South Africa remains firmly committed to strengthening regional stability and security. “Gatherings such as these are not ceremonial engagements they are strategic platforms. They facilitate direct collaboration, intelligence sharing and the development of proactive responses to emerging criminal trends,” he said. He said the meetings were an important tool in dismantling the work of organised crime syndicates. “By coming together under one roof and sharing expertise, we are now better prepared, more capable, and one step ahead in confronting increasingly sophisticated criminal networks. “In South Africa, we are doing all we can to dislodge the work of organised crime syndicates, we continue to intensify efforts against the syndicates operating within the nine identified focus areas through weekly operations,” the General said. Masemola said in terms of addressing the proliferation of firearms, in South Africa authorities conduct stop-and-search operations, roadblocks, tracing initiatives and targeted intelligence-driven interventions. “On average, we seize no fewer than 120 firearms per week. Just this past week, we confiscated 173 firearms, the previous week, 147 and the week before that, 142 unlicensed firearms,” he said. Masemola said they have destroyed 13 859 firearms as part of the annual destruction process. “Over the past seven years, the SAPS has permanently removed more than 305 000 firearms from circulation. These weapons were either seized during operations or voluntarily surrendered,” he said. On drug trafficking, Masemola told the delegates that at OR Tambo International Airport, a primary gateway into Africa, they have arrested more than 20 drug mules in recent operations. “Many originate from São Paulo, Brazil, and smuggle drugs by swallowing them. Through intelligence-led policing, we intercept these individuals and follow due legal and medical processes to recover the drugs. “Most of our arrests through our weekly operations are those that were found in possession of drugs. Just this past four months, we arrested more than 33 000 suspects who were found in possession of drugs,” Masemola said. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3679,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/banyana-star-nominated-caf-award","Pretoria - Banyana Banyana and their Russian-based striker Nompumelelo Nyandeni have both been nominated for the African Football Confederation (CAF) awards. The CAF awards will be held in Accra, Ghana, on Thursday, 22 December. The annual event is the continent's premier awards which recognise the best achievers in African football for the year. The South African Senior Women's National Team has been nominated for the African Women's National Team of the Year category, while Nyandeni will vie for the African Women's Footballer of the Year 2011. Banyana has had a brilliant year and topped it up by qualifying for the 2012 London Olympic Games, while striker Nyandeni has been a revelation for the squad. Other nominees in Banyana's category are Cameroon and Nigeria. Nyandeni, who played a key role in Banyana's qualification for the Olympics next year, will battle it out for the biggest prize with Miriam Paixao Silva of Equatorial Guinea and Perpetua Nkwocha of Nigeria. This year's event will be televised to the global audience and will be attended by the who's who of African football. South African Football Association President, Kirsten Nematandani, said: ""Our Women's National Team has done exceptionally well this year and the nominations are due recognition for an excellent season. ""We hope this will spur them to do even better at the Olympic Games in London."" General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",1529,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/mec-slams-report-tambo-memorial-hospital","Pretoria - Gauteng MEC for Health and Social Development, Qedani Mahlangu, said the statement made on the missing patient's fees at Tambo Memorial Hospital in Ekurhuleni was mischievous and misleading. According to claims reported to the media, it was discovered that some of the missing receipt books were used and monies collected were not deposited by the main cashier. This included more than R150 000 being stolen by unauthorised use of 14 receipt books. Mahlangu said that she finds it odd and astounding that the claims have been made to the media pre-empting the outcome of the investigation, while the matter - which was referred for investigation to the Gauteng Shared Service Centre Security Risk Management Department - has not yet determine how much was stolen. ""It is only fair to allow the forensic investigation to run its course, where after appropriate action will be taken,"" Mahlangu said. She also reiterated the department's commitment to rooting out corruption as it impacts negatively on service delivery. ""We will not hesitate to deal with individuals who engage in corrupt activities within and outside the department."" General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",1292,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/preparations-track-gauteng-matric-exams","Johannesburg - The Gauteng Department of Education says preparations to hold the 2009 matric examinations are at an advanced stage. A total of 101 218 full time and 8117 part time repeater candidates will be sitting for the Senior Certificate Examination in 790 centres across the province on 26 October 2009. Gauteng MEC for Education Barbara Creecy announced on Sunday that the number of registered fulltime candidates was up by 2 234 from last year's total of 99 984. She said this was due to an additional 1316 from the 10 Merafong schools being registered in Gauteng and a marginally improved flow through rate of learners. Practical examinations for Computer Application Technology will start on 7 October 2009 and Information Technology on 9 October 2009. ""We are proud and confident to announce that we will deliver credible examinations to our Grade 12 learners in Gauteng,"" Creecy said. The department has been working closely with South African Police Services to ensure tight security during the printing, storage and delivery of examination papers. ""Printing and packaging of question papers are at an advanced stage, schools will receive their question papers only on the morning when monitored, moderated and marks in these components are being captured. ""Processes have been put in place to ensure that all School Based Assessment marks are correctly submitted,"" she said, adding that a scanning process will be used to record mark sheets submitted so that a follow up on outstanding mark sheets can be monitored and addressed. Creecy reminded learners that no information should be taken into the exam room including cellphones, talking in the exam room and copying from one another She described these as the ""silly things that can compromise your future after 12 years of hard work"". ""Do not be tempted,"" warned Creecy. General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",1986,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/lge2026-results-capturing-making-steady-progress","Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) Chairperson Mosotho Moepya says results capturing and verification are progressing steadily across the country, with more than 60% of the 66 073 results captured at 5pm on Thursday. “The Commission further reports that about 40 202 result slips have been scanned, while 32 238 have undergone independent auditing as part of the verification and quality assurance processes. These measures are aimed at ensuring the accuracy, transparency and integrity of the results before they are finalised,” Moepya said at the National Results Operations Centre (ROC) in Midrand. He was briefing the media on vote counting, results verification and capturing following the 2026 Local Government Elections, held on Wednesday, when South Africans went to the polls to elect representatives to municipal councils. The elections gave citizens an opportunity to influence local governance and hold elected representatives accountable for addressing service delivery challenges and improving quality of life in their communities. Provincially, the Northern Cape had captured 87% of its results, followed by the Western Cape at 69%, the Free State at 64% and the Eastern Cape at 60%. Mpumalanga had captured 52% of its results, followed by KwaZulu-Natal at 37%, North West and Gauteng at 35% each, and Limpopo at 23%. Moepya said 65 of the country’s 213 municipalities (31%) had completed the capturing, scanning, auditing and exception process. The Commission cautioned that the figures remain provisional until objections material to an election have been finalised. “The Commission remains committed to finalising the results as expeditiously as possible, while ensuring that all prescribed verification and quality assurance processes are rigorously followed to safeguard the accuracy, transparency and integrity of the results. It is anticipated that the results collation process will be concluded by the weekend,” the Chairperson said. The Commission urged political parties, independent candidates, the media and the public to exercise caution when interpreting partial results and to rely on the officially verified results it publishes. Issue Tracker The Commission launched an enhanced Issue Tracker for this election, as part of the Virtual ROC. The Virtual ROC provides remote access to electoral operational systems and the 10 Results Operations Centres. “One of the innovations going into the 2026 Local Government Elections has been the enhancement of the Issue Tracker. This is a mechanism for contestants and electoral staff to report all issues that could impair the election process,” Moepya said. The Issue Tracker has resolved more than 1 000 cases logged since special voting began. “The Commission reiterates its call for all stakeholders to continue to observe election regulations pertaining to the results processing phase and avoid issuing disinformation about the results,” the Chairperson said. -SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3110,"60%, 87%, 69%, 64%, 60%, 52%, 37%, 35%, 23%, 31%",10
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/parties-urged-exercise-patience-kzn-election-results-are-captured","The Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) in KwaZulu-Natal has called on political parties, independent candidates and their supporters to exercise patience and allow the counting and results capturing processes to proceed in accordance with established electoral procedures. Provincial Electoral Officer Ntombifuthi Masinga made the appeal on Thursday as the Commission continued processing results from the 2026 Local Government Elections. Masinga also urged political parties and other role players refrain from disseminating unverified information or disinformation regarding election results. “Such conduct has the potential to create unnecessary tensions, undermine public confidence, incite violence and result in damage to property or injury,” Masinga said. Giving an update on the counting process, Masinga said this was progressing smoothly at all voting stations, with no interruptions reported. As at 12pm on Thursday, the Commission had received 4 872 (35%) of the expected 14 245 results slips, while 4 452 (31.3%) had been scanned into the results system. Of the results captured, 3 432 (24.1%) had already undergone independent auditing. Masinga said the Commission had received complete sets of results from two of the province's smaller municipalities -- Endumeni and Emadlangeni. ""These results have been captured; however, they will not be immediately available because they still must go through the auditing process, which is currently underway. The Commission is impressed with the level of capturing in our offices,"" Masinga said. In eThekwini, the province's largest municipality, Area Managers are continuing to transport results from voting stations to the Provincial Results Operations Centre. Commission officials are also working to resolve exceptions flagged by the results system. Masinga explained that the system uses predefined percentage thresholds, with results falling outside these parameters automatically flagged for verification. ""Each flagged result is subjected to further scrutiny to confirm its accuracy and compliance with the prescribed parameters. Where the exception is confirmed to be valid, it is subsequently cleared on the system, allowing the results processing process to proceed,"" she said. Preliminary voter turnout Preliminary figures indicate that KwaZulu-Natal recorded 2 937 250 voters, representing a turnout of 49%. The province recorded the second-highest number of voters nationally, behind Gauteng, which recorded approximately three million voters. The figures are preliminary, as additional data is still being uploaded. Reported incidents Masinga also reported power outages affecting eight voting stations in the KwaMakhutha area in Ward 94, as well as Lugobe High School in Ward 96. In Ward 72, at Shallcross Primary School, an elderly woman passed away while being transported to hospital. The Commission extended its condolences to the family and loved ones of the deceased. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3117,"35%, 3%, 1%, 49%",4
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/election-observer-satisfied-voting-process","Defend Our Democracy (DOD) election observer Thandeka Sithole has expressed satisfaction with the overall conduct of the 2026 Local Government Elections (LGE). Speaking to SAnews.gov.za following Wednesday’s Voting Day, Sithole described the voting process as professional, despite some challenges experienced at voting stations during peak hours. Sithole said she was encouraged by the voter turnout announced by the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC), particularly given concerns that participation could fall below the 2021 LGE. “I was really worried that the turnout might be lower than the 2021 turnout, given the past two or three voter registration weekends that I have been involved in. However, I was really happy with the overall voter turnout,” she told SAnews.gov.za on Thursday. DOD is a South African civil society movement which also promotes an active citizenry. Sithole said the election outcomes are particularly important as South Africa continues to navigate coalition politics, emphasising the need for broad representation and greater transparency in the formation of coalition governments. She emphasised the importance of many voices being represented in municipal councils and political parties understanding how to manage coalition arrangements effectively. “I am also expecting all coalition agreements to be published and accessible to the public, because no deals can be done in secret about us,” she said. On youth participation, Sithole said she observed an encouraging number of young people queuing to vote at several voting stations she visited in the Midrand, Gauteng area. Although she has not yet seen the IEC's official statistics on youth participation, she said her observations were positive. Regarding the administration of the elections, Sithole said she was generally satisfied with the process, but pointed to challenges linked to the Commission’s capacity and resources, with IEC officials at some voting stations having struggled to manage queues during the morning peak period, including inadequate queue marshals and difficulties processing voters at stations operating multiple voting substations. “In some instances, officials took about an hour and a half to get operations settled, leaving community members who had been queuing since 6am frustrated. “Overall, I am satisfied. The process was conducted in a professional way. However, we can still see the underfunding of the IEC within the process,” Sithole told SAnews.gov.za. Sithole called for increased funding for the Commission, particularly to strengthen the training of electoral officials and improve the management of voting stations. - SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2820,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/iec-reports-159-million-election-participants-results-process-continues","The Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) has reported that 15.9 million South Africans participated in the 2026 Local Government Elections, including those who cast special votes, while stressing that preliminary turnout figures and the final results are still being determined. IEC Deputy Chief Electoral Officer Masego Sheburi said the Commission would only be able to provide a breakdown of voter participation, including the number of young and first-time voters, once the counting process has been concluded. “We can indicate that at this rate, 15.9 million South Africans participated both during special votes, as well as during the elections yesterday,” Sheburi said during a Question and Answer session at media briefing on Thursday. Responding to questions about complaints and irregularities, Sheburi said the Commission had received just over 800 complaints by Wednesday night, with most relating to voting station staff allegedly preventing party agents from observing the voting and counting processes. He said the majority of the complaints have been resolved, with approximately 106 remaining open. Sheburi explained that the complaints received by the Commission were distinct from objections dealt with by presiding officers at voting stations. Contestants also have an opportunity to lodge objections that could materially affect the outcome of declared results. These must be submitted by 17:00 on Friday. Sheburi said the Commission would only be in a position to announce the final results once it has dealt with the objections. “The Commission, as we indicated yesterday, has seven days lawfully to declare the result. The Commission does not want to keep the country in angst and for that reason, it will release the result as soon as all objections have been dealt with. “Lawfully, the Commission cannot release or declare the results before 17:00 hours tomorrow afternoon,” Sheburi said. On the circulation of posters claiming that certain political parties have won particular wards, Sheburi cautioned that results displayed at voting stations should be treated as provisional, until formally declared by the Commission. He explained that results slips were completed in duplicate following the counting process at each voting station. One copy had been transported to a capturing site, while the other remained at the voting station as a public announcement of the provisional result. “If it has not been altered or tampered with, treat it as a provisional result, until such time that the Commission announces the final result, because that result could still be subject to an objection that may lead to a recount and that result changes lawfully,” he said. Meanwhile, IEC Vice Chairperson Janet Love urged South Africans to verify information before sharing it, particularly on social media, where unverified election results and misleading claims could spread quickly. She said the Commission has established a platform with civil society and media organisations to monitor and counter disinformation. “We have urged people and continue to urge people to forward posts that they wish to get clarity on, or that they regard as disinformation to the Real411 platform,” Love said. The Commission is also working with major social media platforms to address problematic posts relating to the electoral process. Love encouraged citizens to check information before reposting it, warning against sharing content that is intended to mislead or cause mischief. “Lastly, I think we've really been reaching out quite extensively to everybody, and we wish to do that again, to really urge people -- as has been done today -- to verify information, to check and ensure that we don't just repost things that are plainly mischief-making,” she said. IEC Chief Electoral Officer Sy Mamabolo also explained that the length of ballot papers in metropolitan municipalities had affected the pace of results capturing. He said the eight metros had particularly long ballots, which meant that counting and capturing would take longer. “The reality of the situation is that in all the metros, the ballots were quite long. Now, the practical implications of that is it takes longer to count. It will similarly take longer to capture,” Mamabolo said. He added that the absence of party agents' signatures on results slips did not invalidate them, provided they have been signed by the presiding officer. The Commission said that, as at 09:00 on Thursday, 23 444 of the 66 073 expected results slips had been scanned into the results system, while 22 983 had been captured. Of the captured results slips, 16 791 had undergone independent auditing. Results from 18 local municipalities had been concluded at that stage. The IEC has urged political parties, independent candidates and their supporters to remain patient as the counting, verification and results-capturing processes continue, and to refrain from circulating unverified information about the outcome of the elections. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",5155,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/sa-welcomes-un-decision-advance-global-response-environmental-crime","South Africa has welcomed a decision to strengthen international law enforcement efforts against facilitators of transnational organised environmental crime, advancing the global response to this growing threat. The decision was adopted at the Thirteenth Session of the Conference of the Parties (COP13) to the United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime (UNTOC), held in Vienna, Austria, from 19 to 23 October 2026. “The resolution, adopted with an overwhelming 87.37% of votes cast in favour and co-sponsored by South Africa, marks a significant milestone in international efforts to combat transnational organised environmental crime. It establishes a process to elaborate an additional Protocol to UNTOC addressing illicit trafficking in wildlife and timber, illegal mining and illicit trafficking in minerals and precious metals, and illicit trafficking in waste,” Minister of Forestry, Fisheries and the Environment David Maynier said in a statement on Thursday. South Africa played an important role in advancing this initiative through a coordinated, whole-of-government approach involving the Department of International Relations and Cooperation, the Department of Forestry, Fisheries and the Environment (DFFE), the National Prosecuting Authority (NPA) and the South African Police Service (SAPS). The collaboration combined practical environmental enforcement experience with criminal justice and prosecutorial expertise, alongside diplomatic engagement, to advance a stronger international legal response to environmental crime. “This is a significant achievement for South Africa and the international community. Environmental crime is organized crime, and we need to tackle the complex international networks facilitating illicit wildlife trafficking in coordination with other countries,” the Minister said. The decision builds on momentum generated during South Africa’s 2025 Group of Twenty (G20) Presidency, when the Cape Town Ministerial Declaration on Crimes that Affect the Environment was adopted for the first time. The Declaration, subsequently welcomed by G20 Leaders, recognised the serious threat posed by these crimes and the urgent need to strengthen national responses. It also reinforced international recognition of the importance of addressing environmental crime. Throughout the negotiations, South Africa highlighted the challenges countries face in investigating and prosecuting sophisticated criminal networks. These networks exploit differences in national legislation, weaknesses in cross-border cooperation and legitimate supply chains to facilitate crimes against the environment and conceal the proceeds. An additional Protocol could help address these legal gaps by strengthening criminalisation and international cooperation while respecting national sovereignty and complementing existing international agreements. ""Our experience has demonstrated that combating transnational environmental crime requires an integrated response, both nationally and internationally. The overwhelming support for this resolution represents an important step towards ensuring that our international legal frameworks keep pace with the increasingly sophisticated criminal networks exploiting our natural resources,"" Maynier said. DFFE said the decision builds on extensive international deliberations, including the work of an intergovernmental expert group established under COP12 to examine gaps in existing legal frameworks and identify opportunities to strengthen the international response. “While negotiations on the additional Protocol are underway, South Africa will continue working closely with its international partners to strengthen cross-border cooperation, intelligence sharing, joint investigations and efforts to disrupt the criminal networks and illicit financial flows associated with environmental crime. “These practical partnerships remain essential to delivering meaningful enforcement outcomes while the international legal framework is being strengthened,” the Minister said. He added that the strong support for the resolution marked an important step forward in the global fight against environmental crime. “South Africa remains committed to playing an active role in shaping the additional Protocol, ensuring that the realities faced by enforcement authorities and prosecutors are reflected in an international legal framework capable of delivering meaningful results,” Maynier said. -SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",4622,"37%",1
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/lge-2026-vote-counting-underway","Following a successful voting process on Wednesday, vote counting in the 2026 Local Government Elections (LGE) is underway. Voting stations closed their doors at 9pm on Voting Day, 4 November 2026, with the Electoral Commission expressing confidence that all voters who were in line at the cut-off time were given the opportunity to cast their ballots. “The counting, tallying and verification of votes continued throughout the night with the presence of political party agents, independent candidate agents and accredited observers,” Chief Electoral Officer Sy Mamabolo said. Speaking at a media briefing on Thursday, Mamabolo highlighted the importance of the next stage of the process, which is the results capturing process, as an important phase of the election results management and transmission. “It is important to underscore the procedural tenet of the counting of ballots, which is that counting happens at the voting station where the ballots were cast to ensure that every single ballot paper is accounted for at every station and duly recorded. This is an important watermark of [the] free and fairness of elections. “After counting, a result slip is captured in duplicate in front of party and independent agents, as well as observers. The agents can record and make photographic images of the results slips. Similarly, the IEC makes an image of the results to facilitate retrieval by contestants where this may be required,” he explained. From the voting station, result slips are transported in temper evident envelopes to the local capturing site of the Commission. Capturing involves stringent quality assurance measures, including double blind capturing of the result slips, scanning of the results slips into the results system, and auditing by independent auditors on the total population of results. Meaning that the audit is not sampled, but the entire results are subjected to this important assurance measure. He said as of 9am this morning, 66 073 result slips were expected from the local municipality, metros and district council votes across the 23 696 voting stations countrywide. “About 23 444 (35.5 %) of the expected results slips have been scanned through to the results system, and 22 983 (34.8 %) have been captured. Out of the results slips captured, 16 791 (25.4%) have already been independently audited,” he said. The Commission further added that results from 18 local municipalities (7%) had been concluded at 9am. “In a comparative election in 2021 at the same time, only seven municipalities were completed. The improved rate of capturing is in spite of the increased lengths of ballot paper, higher number of contestants and higher rate of voter participation,” said the Commission. Call for patience The Commission appealed to political parties, independent candidates and their supporters to exercise patience and allow the counting and results-capturing processes to unfold. “The Commission further urges all candidates and political roleplayers to refrain from spreading unverified information or disinformation about election results, as this could create unnecessary tensions, incite violence and potentially result in damage to property and injuries,” Mamabolo explained. The Commission has invited the public, media and political contestants to follow the results on the following IEC Platforms: • Website: www.elections.org.za • IEC Mobile App The Commission also maintains regular updates on its WhatsApp channel (0600 88 0000). The briefing came as political parties kept watch over results coming in. READ | Parties keep close watch as election results trickle in Meanwhile, government has thanked voters for casting their ballots in the elections. READ | Government thanks South Africans for participating in elections General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3925,"4%, 7%",2
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/kzn-records-487-voter-turnout-results-capturing-continues","KwaZulu-Natal has recorded a voter turnout of 48.7% in the 2026 Local Government Elections, exceeding the national average of 45.8%, the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) in the province says. Provincial Electoral Officer Ntombifuthi Masinga on Thursday said the commission was encouraged by the turnout, although it had hoped the province would surpass the 50% mark. “We are quite pleased with what appears to be a higher than the national average voter turnout in our province,” Masinga said during a media briefing, following the closure of voting stations on Wednesday night. She thanked voters who turned out to cast their ballots, saying the commission was still awaiting the capture of outstanding results. Masinga said at the time of the briefing, only 18% of the results had been captured, while 12% had gone through the auditing process. She explained that auditing is a critical part of the results management process, as it independently verifies that the results captured on the commission’s system correspond with those recorded at voting stations. “Until the results have been audited by an independent firm of auditors to confirm that what we have in our system matches what came out of the voting station, that result is not available on the dashboard,” she said. She noted that political parties might appear to be further ahead in reporting results because their agents were encouraged to photograph results slips at voting stations and submit them to their respective party results collation centres. However, unlike political parties, the IEC is required to subject results to an independent audit before making them available on its dashboard. The commission is now focusing on completing the capturing and auditing of results, while political parties and independent candidates have until 5pm on Friday to lodge objections in terms of Section 65 of the Electoral Act. Masinga said these objections could relate to any aspect of the electoral process where a party or candidate believed that the integrity of results from a particular voting station might have been compromised. The commission has appointed a panel of legal practitioners to assist with investigating the objections received before the deadline. She said the IEC would only announce the final election results on Sunday after all objections had been considered and resolved. Following the announcement, the commission has seven days from the date of voting to publish the election results in the Government Gazette. The election of municipal council office-bearers, including mayors and speakers, as well as representatives to district councils, must then take place within 14 days of the publication of the results. - SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2878,"7%, 8%, 50%, 18%, 12%",5
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/revised-gdp-figures-improve-key-fiscal-ratios","National Treasury says Statistics South Africa’s (Stats SA) 2026 rebasing and benchmarking of gross domestic product (GDP) data has increased the measured level of nominal GDP by 5.1% (R393 billion). In a statement on Thursday, National Treasury said it had updated key fiscal ratios, following the GDP revision announced by Stats SA on 29 October 2026. The updated ratios reflect a mechanical adjustment based on Stats SA’s revised GDP data. “Based on Stats SA’s updated numbers, the debt to Gross Domestic Product ratio peaked at 74.6% in 2025/26, down from the 78.4% estimated in the 2026 Medium Term Budget Policy Statement (MTBPS). The debt-to-GDP ratio falls below 70% by 2032/33, a year earlier than forecast in the MTBPS,” National Treasury said. Other key fiscal ratios have also declined. The primary surplus is now 1.6% of GDP in 2026/27, compared with 1.7% in the MTBPS, rising to 2.8% in 2029/30, compared with 2.9%. The main budget deficit for 2026/27 is now 3.5% of GDP, compared with 3.7%. National Treasury said the revised ratios do not reflect changes to government’s revenue, spending or debt projections. The 2027 Budget will provide further details, including updated economic forecasts and fiscal projections. “Our updated calculations confirm that government continues to achieve its fiscal targets, with the fiscal metrics looking even better than we had projected based on Statistics South Africa’s revised GDP data. We are restoring fiscal credibility and South Africa is seeing the benefits in lower borrowing costs and a stronger foundation for investment and growth,” National Treasury Director-General Duncan Pieterse said. The benchmarking exercise uses improved data and methods to incorporate major changes in the economy’s structure into GDP calculations, ensuring they more accurately reflect its size and composition. “Historically, Stats SA’s five-yearly benchmarking exercises have often increased the estimated size of the economy - the absolute level of GDP – but have seldom impacted economic growth rates meaningfully,” National Treasury said. The higher nominal GDP figure reduces fiscal ratios that use it as the denominator. On average, the consolidated budget deficit improves by 0.2 percentage points of GDP for the current fiscal year and over the medium term. The main budget primary surplus is revised down by an average of 0.1 percentage points over the medium term. -SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2581,"1%, 6%, 4%, 70%, 6%, 7%, 8%, 9%, 5%, 7%",10
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/parties-keep-close-watch-election-results-trickle","The Electoral Commission of South Africa’s (IEC) national Results Operations Centre (ROC) in Midrand is abuzz with activity, as political parties closely monitor the first results from the 2026 Local Government Elections. Party representatives have gathered at the centre as preliminary results started trickling in this morning, following Wednesday’s voting, with parties hoping for a strong showing in the contest for control of municipalities across the country. The IEC registered approximately 29.2 million voters for the elections, with voting taking place at 23,696 voting stations. Special voting was conducted on Monday and Tuesday ahead of Election Day. The Commission will be briefing the media at 10am. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",878,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/government-thanks-south-africans-participating-elections","Government has expressed its sincere appreciation to all South Africans, especially registered voters who turned out in large numbers to exercise their democratic right to vote in the 2026 Local Government Elections. “While we celebrate the participation of millions of South Africans in this important democratic process, government conveys its deepest condolences to the families who lost their loved ones during the electioneering period. We also wish those affected strength and comfort during this difficult time,” Deputy Government Spokesperson William Baloyi said. This comes as National Police Commissioner Fannie Masemola on Wednesday said police are dealing with serious crimes, including murders reported in KwaZulu-Natal and the Eastern Cape. READ | Police investigate 14 election-related cases South Africans went to the polls on Wednesday for the Local Government Elections to elect representatives to municipal councils. The elections allowed citizens to influence local governance and hold elected representatives accountable for addressing service delivery challenges and improving quality of life in communities. Government also commended the Independent Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) for its continued efforts to ensure a free, fair and credible election. “Government reaffirms its confidence in the IEC and recognises its important role in administering the country's electoral processes and safeguarding democracy. “Government also extends its appreciation to law enforcement agencies who worked tirelessly to maintain peace, stability and public order throughout the election period,” Baloyi said in a statement on Wednesday night. Government further thanked all employers who released employees to vote and acknowledged individuals and organisations that, through acts of kindness and generosity, assisted voters while they waited in queues to cast their ballots. “These gestures of goodwill reflect the spirit of Ubuntu. As the country concludes this important democratic exercise, government calls on all South Africans to continue upholding the principles of unity and social cohesion. “Democracy does not end at the ballot box; it requires continued citizen participation, accountability and constructive engagement to ensure that local government delivers on its mandate,” he said. Government reiterated its commitment to working with all sectors of society to strengthen local governance, advance inclusive development and build safe, sustainable and resilient communities. -SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2678,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/police-take-action-voting-day-linked-cases","Police have opened 57 dockets related to some 85 incidents that occurred on Voting Day of the Local Government Elections. This was revealed by co-chairperson of the National Joint Operational and Intelligence Structure (NATJOINTS), Lieutenant General Tebello Mosikili, who briefed the media on behalf of the body on Thursday evening. On Voting Day, some 85 incidents were reported across the country, with 57 case dockets registered and 27 suspects arrested. The provincial distribution is as follows: · Eastern Cape: 10 cases, four arrests. · Free State: two cases, two arrests. · Gauteng: eight cases, seven arrests. · KwaZulu-Natal: 15 cases, six arrests. · Limpopo: 11 cases, four arrests. · Mpumalanga: three cases, three arrests. · North West: three cases, no arrests at this stage. · Northern Cape: one case, one arrest. · Western Cape: four cases, no arrests at this stage. “These included 32 cases relating to contraventions of the Electoral Act, four assaults, two cases of malicious damage to property, two intimidation cases, one theft, two cases of crimen injuria, one case of drunk and disorderly conduct, one public violence case, one business burglary and one fraud case. “The fact that 27 suspects were arrested on Voting Day demonstrates the responsiveness and operational effectiveness of law enforcement agencies. “These interventions reinforce a clear position that the electoral environment is protected by the rule of law, and intimidation, violence, electoral misconduct and criminality will not be tolerated,” Mosikili said. On Special Voting days – Monday and Tuesday – some 65 criminal case dockets were registered, with 32 suspects arrested. The provincial breakdown of registered cases is as follows: · Eastern Cape: eight cases registered, with three suspects arrested. · Free State: four cases registered, with no arrests yet. · Gauteng: seven cases registered, with three suspects arrested. · KwaZulu-Natal: 16 cases registered, with eight suspects arrested. · Limpopo: 13 cases registered, with 11 suspects arrested. · Mpumalanga: five cases registered, with four suspects arrested. · North West: four cases registered, with three suspects arrested. · Northern Cape: zero cases. · Western Cape: eight cases registered, no arrests. “These figures demonstrate that the security response was not limited to maintaining visibility and preventing incidents. It extended to active law enforcement, the apprehension of suspects, investigation and the advancement of criminal matters through the justice system. “NATJOINTS remains resolute that violations of electoral legislation and other criminal conduct will be dealt with decisively, irrespective of where they occur or who is involved,” Mosikili emphasised. Cracking cases During the election period, police registered 21 cases of murder, including the recent murder of an MK Party member in KwaZulu-Natal. “Meanwhile, while commencing the post-election phase of the plan, police are also processing murder cases that occurred outside the voting stations and outside voting hours in Bushbuckridge, Mpumalanga, where a 57-year-old man was shot and killed in the early hours of [Thursday] morning. “In another unrelated incident in Limpopo … police registered a murder case … following a stabbing at a park opposite Florapark Comprehensive School in Polokwane. A swift police response resulted in the arrest of the 35-year-old man in connection with the murder. “Another case of murder was reported in KwaZulu-Natal of a man who was attacked and killed while walking home on Voting Day,” Mosikili said. Furthermore, cooperation between law enforcement and the IEC enabled swift interventions in reported incidents, including taking action against IEC officials implicated in alleged electoral transgressions. “SAPS also acted against a member in KwaZulu-Natal, who arrived at a voting station in a vehicle displaying political party branding, which is testament to our institutional position that no individual is above the law,” Mosikili said. READ | IEC terminates KZN presiding officer's services over election material transportation Mosikili reiterated that law enforcement continues its work to protect the integrity of the elections. “We thank our law enforcement personnel, government departments, the IEC and all stakeholders whose cooperation contributed to maintaining a safe electoral environment. We also commend communities and eligible voters for participating in the democratic process. “The Security Cluster remains on the ground, alert and operationally ready until the electoral process is concluded,” Mosikili concluded. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",4783,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/police-investigate-14-election-related-cases","The South African Police Service (SAPS) is investigating approximately 14 cases linked to the 2026 Local Government Elections, ranging from theft and intimidation, to incidents involving political party posters. Speaking to the media during a doorstep interview on his arrival at the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) Results Operations Centre in Midrand on Wednesday, National Police Commissioner Fannie Masemola said the cases are being investigated as police maintain security during the elections. “Since yesterday, most of the cases are related to the elections -- either theft of ballot papers and intimidation, or fighting where there is intolerance among political party members,” Masemola said. Masemola said police are also dealing with serious crimes, including murders reported in KwaZulu-Natal and the Eastern Cape. He said areas identified as election hotspots include parts of eThekwini and uMkhanyakude in KwaZulu-Natal, as well as some areas in the Eastern and Western Cape. Despite these concerns, the Commissioner said the elections have generally proceeded peacefully, with security arrangements in place to respond to incidents, where necessary. In one incident in Amathole District Municipality, a protest over water-related grievances required police intervention. Masemola said the protest was dispersed to allow voting to continue. While acknowledging the community’s right to raise concerns, Masemola stressed the need to ensure that the voting process is not disrupted. He reiterated the importance of police officers remaining politically impartial during the elections. He said officers are entitled to vote for the political party of their choice in their personal capacity but are not permitted to wear political party regalia while on duty, as this can compromise the perception of impartiality. Citizens urged to exercise their democratic right Meanwhile, Director-General in the Presidency Phindile Baleni called on South Africans to use their remaining time to vote, highlighting the direct impact of local government decisions on people’s daily lives. She said municipal government is responsible for issues that directly affect communities, including water, electricity, roads, and local economic development. “Firstly, local government is about the environment in which we live. It is about our livelihood. It is about the basics. It is about the water, electricity, the roads and the economy of the local areas in which we live,” Baleni said. She said voting is both a democratic right and a responsibility, given the sacrifices made by those who fought for South Africa’s democracy. “The right to participate in democracy comes with the responsibility to vote and therefore, it is important that we vote. Otherwise, if we do not vote, nobody will ever hear our voice, and the issues we care about will not be attended to,” she said. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3045,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/voters-urged-use-remaining-time-cast-their-ballots","With just under two hours remaining before voting stations close at 9pm, the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) has urged eligible voters, who have not yet cast their votes, to use the remaining time to participate in the 2026 Local Government Elections. IEC Chief Electoral Officer Sy Mamabolo said voting has progressed well across the country, despite a few isolated incidents. Mamabolo reassured voters that anyone who arrives at their registered voting station before 9pm will be allowed to vote, even if the process extends beyond the official closing time. “This means that voters who are in the queue by 9pm will not be turned away. We, however, encourage voters not to leave it until the last minute, but to use the remaining time to visit voting stations,” Mamabolo said. As voting enters the evening period, the Commission has made arrangements to ensure that generators and alternative lighting facilities are available at voting stations in areas experiencing electricity outages. This aims to allow the counting process to proceed without unnecessary disruption. Preliminary voter turnout According to preliminary figures, as at 5pm, approximately 12 million voters had cast their ballots. Gauteng recorded the highest number of voters, at three million, followed by KwaZulu-Natal with 2.5 million, Western Cape 1.5 million, and the Eastern Cape with 1.4 million. Limpopo recorded 1.1 million voters, Mpumalanga 854 901, North West 702 133, the Free State 588 237, and the Northern Cape 299 381. Mamabolo said the Commission is encouraged by the turnout recorded at many voting stations across the country. Counting and results process Mamabolo reported that the counting of ballots will begin at each voting station shortly after voting closes. He explained that ballot papers will be counted at the voting stations where they were cast, in the presence of political party agents, independent candidate agents and accredited observers. “Sealed ballot boxes are opened, and ballot papers are checked for the Electoral Commission's security stamp. Ballots are then sorted and counted separately for each electoral contest, namely the proportional representation (PR) ballot, ward candidate ballot and, where applicable, the district council PR ballot. “Following the count, the results for each contest are reconciled against the ballot papers received, issued, unused, spoilt and counted at the voting station. Results slips are captured in duplicate, signed by the Presiding Officer, and countersigned by party agents before being displayed at the voting station as soon as counting and tallying of results is completed,” Mamabolo explained. He noted that party agents will be allowed to take pictures of the signed result slips of each voting station. The results will subsequently be scanned and captured at local IEC offices, where they will undergo verification and independent external auditing. “The Results Operations Centres provide political parties, independent candidates, observers and the media with an opportunity to monitor the capturing, verification and processing of results, reinforcing the transparency and integrity of the process. “Once counting, capturing and verification have been completed and outstanding queries resolved, the Electoral Commission will declare the final results and announce the allocation of seats in municipal councils within the prescribed seven-day period,” Mamabolo explained. The Commission has thanked political parties and independent candidates participating in the elections, particularly those represented through the National Political Liaison Committee (NPLC), Provincial Political Liaison Committees (PPLCs) and Local Political Liaison Committees (LPLCs). “These structures have provided an important platform for continuous engagement, consultation and the resolution of issues arising throughout the electoral process.” The Commission also expressed appreciation to voters, election officials, security agencies, observers, the media and other stakeholders for their contributions to the 2026 Local Government Elections. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",4267,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/ntuli-urges-citizens-vote-leaders-who-understand-their-needs","KwaZulu-Natal Premier Thamsanqa Ntuli has urged eligible voters to participate in the 2026 Local Government Elections, saying their votes determine who represents their interests and addresses their service delivery needs at the municipal level. Ntuli made the call after casting his vote at his home in eVumanhlamvu, Nkandla, on Wednesday, emphasising that Local Government Elections give communities an opportunity to elect councillors who understand their needs and aspirations. “I am at home waiting for my councillor to provide me with my needs. It is important to vote because in five years, you may be served by someone you did not vote for and who has no relationship with your needs,” Ntuli said. The Premier said eligible voters, who choose not to participate in voting, risk allowing others to determine who will represent them, even if those elected do not reflect their preferences or priorities. He urged those who had become disillusioned with the electoral process not to withdraw from it, arguing that voting is an important way of ensuring that elected representatives understand the needs of their communities. “Some people say they no longer want to vote because they did not benefit anything. The truth is that if you do not go to vote, you are allowing other people to vote on your behalf and end up voting someone you do not want and not close to your needs,” Ntuli said. He stressed that local government is important. “Municipal Elections are very important because the provincial and national levels recognise us, but it is not the same as the councillors.” He added that voting is essential to sustain planned development initiatives and ensure continued progress in improving people's lives. He acknowledged that communities continue to face challenges and have aspirations that still need to be addressed but said there are opportunities to identify priorities and build on progress already made. The Premier reiterated call for citizens to use their democratic right to elect leaders who will represent their interests and work towards improving service delivery in their communities. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2278,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/young-voters-see-ballot-chance-shape-south-africas-future","Young South Africans have described voting as an important opportunity to influence the country’s future, calling on their peers to participate in the 2026 Local Government Elections and use their voices to bring about change in their communities. Voters who spoke to SAnews at the Nederduitsch Gereformeerde Kerk voting station in Faerie Glen and Sunnyside, Pretoria, on Wednesday, said casting their ballots was both a democratic right and a responsibility. For Elzina Schulte, who voted at the Faerie Glen voting station, participating in the elections was particularly important for young people who wanted to have a say in the direction of the country. “I think it's very important to vote as youth of South Africa. I believe we do have hope and voting means that we have a say in what our country and our future look like. So, I'm very grateful that we can do that today,” she said. Werner Schulte, who also voted at the Faerie Glen station, echoed the call, saying South Africans should not take their democratic rights for granted. “I also believe it's our democratic right to come out and cast our vote. People fought for this freedom, so get out there [and vote]. We have hope in South Africa. Let's restore South Africa and let's make it a country worth fighting for,” he said. He urged young people to stand together and make informed choices at the polls. “I think it’s important for the youth of this country to go out and vote because we all fight together; we all need to stand united. So, let's go out. Let's make a wise decision and let's fight together for this country,” he said. In Sunnyside, 28-year-old Larrayne Rangolo described voting as an opportunity to contribute towards the changes she wanted to see in the country. She encouraged young people to turn out and participate, while acknowledging that meaningful change takes time. “If you don't cast your vote, it's the same as not wanting change. Casting my vote is actually a great opportunity for me to see the changes that I want happening out there, and hopefully they will happen by me casting my vote,” she said. Rangolo said she hoped the changes young people were seeking would contribute to a brighter future. “Everything takes time, but I hope those changes that we're looking for will come forward, and hopefully our future will be brighter than we have right now,” she said. She also described the voting process as straightforward, saying officials were available to guide voters through the process. “The process inside the voting centre was very simple for me. There is guidance, there are people helping you there. So, everything was simple and easy. You don't have to stress at all,” she said. Meanwhile, Hope Molokomme, who also voted in Sunnyside, said voting was an important way for residents to make their concerns heard, particularly around unemployment, limited opportunities and poor service delivery. “We always complain about lack of opportunities and poor service delivery, and for us to be heard, for our voices to be heard, we need to make a move, do better and come and vote,” she said. She urged registered voters who had not yet cast their ballots to make their mark, while encouraging those who had not registered to participate in the next elections. “So, if you haven't registered, it's too late; do better next time, but if you have done so, please go out and make your mark,” she said. The 2026 Local Government Elections provide registered voters with an opportunity to elect the public representatives who will serve their communities and shape the delivery of services at the local government level. Voting stations across the country will remain open until 9 pm, giving registered voters time to cast their ballots before polls close. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",3918,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/iec-enters-partnership-counter-disinformation","The Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) has entered into a partnership with Google, Meta, Microsoft, TikTok and non-profit organisation, Moxii Africa, to ensure the integrity of election information and counter disinformation ahead of the 4 November 2026 Local Government Elections. The partnership is a sequel to a similar framework signed with the platforms ahead of the 2024 National and Provincial Elections (NPE). It is an indispensable lever buttressing the Commission’s purposive measures to enhance the integrity of election information. These measures include the gazetting of the Electoral Code of Conduct Countering Disinformation in the Local Government Elections (the Disinformation Code), on 4 September 2026. The Code applies to political parties, candidates and anyone acting on their behalf. It forms part of the Electoral Code of Conduct and carries the same sanctions. According to the Commission, the partnership brings the platforms into the same effort to keep election information accurate and trustworthy. Underscoring the importance of the partnership, the Commission’s Chief Electoral Officer Sy Mamabolo said the strategic inclusion of the online platforms gives the Commission direct access to the platforms and a real fighting chance against disinformation. “Our partnership with digital platforms is critical to ensuring that voters have access to credible, accurate and trustworthy information to make informed political choices. A technically flawless election can still lose public trust if citizens are overwhelmed by disinformation. “Therefore, the partnership eliminates barriers to direct access to the platforms and enables instantaneous remedial action against disinformation peddlers,” Mamabolo said. The partnership covers the following: Cooperation on other initiatives intended to curb the impact of disinformation in elections. The Commission has designated the Real411 platform to support its proactive efforts to minimise the impact of disinformation during the 2026 Local Government Elections. Anyone who sees suspected election-related disinformation can report it to Real411 at real411.org.za or through the Real411 app. Complaints are handled in all eleven official languages. Real411 assesses complaints independently and recommends action to the Commission and the platforms. Depending on the findings, the Commission, upon careful review of any reported complaint indicating disinformation or misinformation, can: The partners welcomed the partnership and reaffirmed their commitment to working with the Commission. They noted that they already have policies and mechanisms to counter disinformation and will keep using them as part of the collective effort. “Free, fair and credible elections depend on people being able to trust the information they see. No single organisation can tackle disinformation alone, which is why Microsoft is pleased to join the Electoral Commission in this partnership. “Ahead of the 2026 Local Government Elections, we will work alongside the Commission, civil society and industry to help voters find authoritative election information, strengthen media and AI literacy, and counter deceptive AI-generated content,” said Director: Government Affairs, Microsoft South Africa, Abongile Vanda in affirming Microsoft’s support for the partnership. “At TikTok, we recognise the important role our platform plays in helping people discover information and engage in public conversations. This is why we take our responsibility to protect our community during elections very seriously. “Through our in-app Election Centre, South Africans will be able to access reliable information about the elections, and to further support this, we have introduced election-related labels and search banners that direct users to the centre with a single tap. “This work is supported by advanced moderation, collaboration with fact-checking partners and electoral commissions like the IEC South Africa to ensure a safe, informed, and authentic election experience for our community,” TikTok’s Government Relations and Public Policy Lead for Southern Africa, Tholoana Ncheke-Mahlaela, said. “Information integrity is fundamental to the credibility of any democratic process. In an increasingly complex digital environment, protecting voters from misinformation and disinformation requires coordinated action across institutions, technology platforms, civil society and citizens themselves. “This partnership represents an important commitment to ensuring that South Africans can access accurate, trustworthy election information and participate in the 2026 Local Government Elections with confidence,” Moxii Africa Director William Bird welcoming the partnership. Bird said he is proud to support this collective effort to strengthen trust, transparency and democratic resilience. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",5014,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/index.php/south-africa/parties-keep-close-watch-election-results-trickle","The Electoral Commission of South Africa’s (IEC) national Results Operations Centre (ROC) in Midrand is abuzz with activity, as political parties closely monitor the first results from the 2026 Local Government Elections. Party representatives have gathered at the centre as preliminary results started trickling in this morning, following Wednesday’s voting, with parties hoping for a strong showing in the contest for control of municipalities across the country. The IEC registered approximately 29.2 million voters for the elections, with voting taking place at 23,696 voting stations. Special voting was conducted on Monday and Tuesday ahead of Election Day. The Commission will be briefing the media at 10am. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",878,"None",0
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/index.php/south-africa/kzn-records-487-voter-turnout-results-capturing-continues","KwaZulu-Natal has recorded a voter turnout of 48.7% in the 2026 Local Government Elections, exceeding the national average of 45.8%, the Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) in the province says. Provincial Electoral Officer Ntombifuthi Masinga on Thursday said the commission was encouraged by the turnout, although it had hoped the province would surpass the 50% mark. “We are quite pleased with what appears to be a higher than the national average voter turnout in our province,” Masinga said during a media briefing, following the closure of voting stations on Wednesday night. She thanked voters who turned out to cast their ballots, saying the commission was still awaiting the capture of outstanding results. Masinga said at the time of the briefing, only 18% of the results had been captured, while 12% had gone through the auditing process. She explained that auditing is a critical part of the results management process, as it independently verifies that the results captured on the commission’s system correspond with those recorded at voting stations. “Until the results have been audited by an independent firm of auditors to confirm that what we have in our system matches what came out of the voting station, that result is not available on the dashboard,” she said. She noted that political parties might appear to be further ahead in reporting results because their agents were encouraged to photograph results slips at voting stations and submit them to their respective party results collation centres. However, unlike political parties, the IEC is required to subject results to an independent audit before making them available on its dashboard. The commission is now focusing on completing the capturing and auditing of results, while political parties and independent candidates have until 5pm on Friday to lodge objections in terms of Section 65 of the Electoral Act. Masinga said these objections could relate to any aspect of the electoral process where a party or candidate believed that the integrity of results from a particular voting station might have been compromised. The commission has appointed a panel of legal practitioners to assist with investigating the objections received before the deadline. She said the IEC would only announce the final election results on Sunday after all objections had been considered and resolved. Following the announcement, the commission has seven days from the date of voting to publish the election results in the Government Gazette. The election of municipal council office-bearers, including mayors and speakers, as well as representatives to district councils, must then take place within 14 days of the publication of the results. - SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2878,"7%, 8%, 50%, 18%, 12%",5
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/index.php/south-africa/iec-achieves-91-completion-rate-local-election-results","The Electoral Commission (IEC) has achieved a 91% national result completion rate for the 2026 Local Government Elections as at 5 pm on Friday. “The Electoral Commission reports that it continues to make significant progress in finalising the results for the 2026 Local Government Elections,” the Commission said. The Commission expects to receive a total of 66 073 result slips from 23 696 voting stations. Results have been finalised in 150 of the 213 municipalities, excluding the 44 District Councils. All municipalities in the Northern Cape have complete results. Completion rates by province are as follows: The Commission said the remaining results are mostly in metropolitan municipal areas, where population density means there are more voting districts. It also noted that participation by contestants has resulted in longer ballots and result slips. “The Electoral Commission reminds all stakeholders that results remain provisional until all objections and verification processes have been concluded,” the Commission said. It urged political parties, independent candidates, the media and the public to rely only on officially verified results published by the Commission. The Commission also dismissed social media claims that results for Wards 4 and 23 in Emalahleni Local Municipality, Mpumalanga, were being withheld because ballots cast exceeded the number of registered voters. “This claim is false because the official voter turnout in the cited wards is just over 50%, well below the registered voter population,” the Commission said. It also said another claim from the same source, that a municipal official voted four times, was misleading. According to the Commission, the voter went to the wrong voting district in her ward, was scanned there, and was then directed to her correct voting district. “Voting more than once is not possible in the 2026 Local Government Elections because a voter may only cast a ballot in the voting district in which they are registered,” it said. The Commission has also called on citizens to verify social media claims before sharing them, warning that spreading false information can breach the Electoral Code of Conduct and the associated Disinformation Code. It reiterated its committed to finalising results as quickly as possible while safeguarding the accuracy, transparency and integrity of the electoral process. – SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",2541,"91%, 50%",2
"https://www.sanews.gov.za/index.php/south-africa/smooth-start-2026-local-government-elections","The Electoral Commission of South Africa (IEC) has reported a smooth start to the 2026 Local Government Elections, with 98% of the country’s 23 696 voting stations opened by 7 am on Wednesday. Giving an update on voting proceedings at a media briefing in the afternoon, IEC Deputy Chief Electoral Officer, Masego Sheburi, said voting is proceeding well across the country, with millions of registered voters already having cast their ballots. As at 10am, preliminary figures indicated that 3 523 691 voters had cast their votes across the nine provinces, with Gauteng recording the highest number of votes cast, at 895 384, followed by KwaZulu-Natal, with 623 380, the Western Cape, with 461 264, and the Eastern Cape, with 431 123. Limpopo recorded 366 378 votes, Mpumalanga 252 859, North West 204 736, Free State 195 537, and Northern Cape 93 030. Sheburi said the Commission is encouraged by the enthusiasm demonstrated by voters, particularly those who arrived at voting stations in the early hours of the morning. “The Commission urges all the remaining registered voters to use the remaining hours of voting to cast their ballots and exercise their democratic right to participate in the 2026 Local Government Elections,” Sheburi said at the Results Operations Centre (ROC) at Gallagher Convention Centre in Midrand. Sheburi also noted additional measures introduced by IEC the to improve efficiency, including, including handheld barcode scanners attached to Voter Management Devices (VMDs) and sequence numbers on the voters’ roll to speed up voter verification and reduce the time spent at voting stations. “The Voter Management Devices are functioning optimally, supporting the smooth processing and verification of voters at voting stations,” Sheburi said. However, the Commission reported that a few voting stations opened by 9 am due to localised protests and access challenges caused by heavy rain in the Eastern Cape and KwaZulu-Natal. “All ballot papers have since been delivered to voting stations across the country, with only a few deliveries completed shortly after voting commenced.” Voter information The Commission has reminded voters that the number of ballot papers they receive depends on the type of municipality in which they are registered. Unlike the National and Provincial Elections, Sheburi explained that the number of ballot papers issued in the Local Government Elections depends on the type of municipality. “Voters in local municipalities will receive three ballot papers: a grey ballot to elect a ward councillor, a green ballot to vote for a political party in the local council, and a yellow ballot to elect political party representatives to the district council. “Voters in metropolitan municipalities will receive two ballot papers: a grey ballot for their 3 ward councillors and a green ballot for their preferred political party in the metropolitan council,” Sheburi said. He also reminded voters to make one clear cross in the designated box next to their preferred candidate or political party on each ballot paper. “Only one candidate or political party may be selected per ballot. Marking more than one choice, or making additional marks may result in the ballot being declared spoilt.” Ballot papers stolen in KZN The Commission received a report of the theft of unused ballot papers and a stamp in one voting station in Ward 2 in eThekwini, KwaZulu-Natal. “There are 20 voting stations in that ward. We have put measures to ensure that should those ballots surface at any of those voting stations, we will be able to identify, because it is a requirement that a ballot must be validated by a stamp at the back. “A case has been opened with the South African Police Service (SAPS), and our staff has been relieved of their duty for their dereliction at that voting station,” Sheburi said. The Commission also acknowledged the Department of Home Affairs for making its offices available to members of the public to collect identity documents and apply for IDs, as well as agencies and security structures operating through the National Joint Operational and Intelligence Structure (NATJOINTS) for their continued efforts to maintain a safe and secure electoral environment. - SAnews.gov.za General Enquiries newsfiles@gcis.gov.za Tel: 012 473 0213 Editor Roze Britz roze@gcis.gov.za Editor-in-Chief Zanele Mngadi zanelemngadi@gcis.gov.za",4388,"98%",1
